QuickJS on generated9

Build and install inside Plan 9:

    cd /sys/src/cmd/6c
    mk
    mk test
    mk install
    cd /sys/src/cmd/quickjs
    mk clean
    mk
    mk test
    mk install

The build uses pcc, the native compiler/linker, and APE. It bootstraps qjsc,
compiles the upstream JavaScript REPL to C, then links qjs. There is no Go or
host compiler dependency. Binaries are installed in /$objtype/bin.

The amd64 compiler must include generated9's unordered-comparison fix. The
bootstrap distribution's 6c mishandles NaN comparisons; the first four commands
above rebuild it. Rebuild existing QuickJS objects afterward. The QuickJS build
checks NaN equality and fails if the compiler still produces the wrong result.

Examples:

    qjs -e 'print(2n ** 100n)'
    qjs program.js
    qjs -m module.js
    qjs --std -e 'print(os.platform); print(std.loadFile("/dev/sysname"))'

qjs with no arguments starts the upstream REPL. It uses ANSI terminal escape
sequences: use the serial terminal or vt under rio. Type \q to leave it.
Scripts can import the built-in "std" and "os" modules for files, processes,
environment variables, pipes and timers. os.platform is "plan9". Use /bin/rc
or /bin/ape/sh when a script needs an explicit shell; /bin/sh is not assumed.

Ray tracer:

    cd /sys/src/cmd/quickjs
    qjs examples/raytrace.js > /tmp/spheres.ppm
    page /tmp/spheres.ppm

Run page inside rio. The script renders three spheres over a checkerboard,
with shadows, reflections and four samples per pixel. It writes a binary PPM
to standard output, using only the built-in std module. The default size is
480 by 300; supply width and height to change it, for example:

    qjs examples/raytrace.js 800 500 > /tmp/spheres.ppm

qjsc -c produces bytecode in C; qjsc -e adds a main function. The latter can
be compiled with the same pcc flags and linked with the objects in OFILES in
mkfile, as exercised by mk test. Automatic executable generation by qjsc is
not configured. Bytecode must be rebuilt with the corresponding engine version.

Scope and limits

This port is validated on generated9/amd64. Other architectures have not been
validated. The upstream language and module implementation is retained; this
is not a Node.js runtime and does not provide Node or browser APIs.

Workers, Atomics and dynamic C modules (.so) are unavailable. The event loop
uses APE select, whose descriptor range is 0..95; registrations outside that
range throw. APE reports writable descriptors as ready without checking pipe
capacity, so a large write can still block. Use small writes or separate
processes where this matters.

Filesystem behavior follows Plan 9 and APE: symbolic links are unavailable,
stat has second-resolution timestamps and no blocks property. Local timezone
conversion inherits APE's 32-bit time_t range. Terminal dimensions are not
reported. std.urlGet requires an external curl, which this port does not supply.

Validation

mk test runs the unmodified upstream closure, language, loop, BigInt, cyclic
import and built-in tests. test_plan9.js covers numeric edge cases, UTF-8/JSON
files, metadata, binary I/O, environment inheritance, process status, pipes,
descriptor validation and timers. The runner also checks failures for uncaught
exceptions, recursion and memory limits, and compiles/runs qjsc-generated C.

The full upstream test_std.js assumes Unix shell paths and symbolic links.
test_rw_handler.js opens more descriptors than APE select supports. Those tests
and the worker/dynamic-module tests are not included in mk test. The complete
Test262 suite has not been run; this is not a claim of full conformance.

See doc/quickjs.html for the upstream API manual and UPSTREAM for exact sources,
local changes and license provenance.
